    <h3>Concentration vs. Dispersion</h3>
    <p>The 1950s stand out as the most concentrated decade: Dimitri Tiomkin alone won three of ten awards, and five of the decade's seven unique winners were repeat champions from prior decades. This was the era of the established Hollywood composer. By contrast, the 1940s and 1980s — both with 12 unique winners — were the most diffuse, driven in part by multiple-composer credits (<em>Stagecoach</em> in 1939 spilling into the era's count, and <em>The Last Emperor</em> in 1987 adding three names in a single year).</p>
    <p>The 2010s achieved a Diversity Index of 1.00 — every ceremony had a new lead face on the podium, with Alexandre Desplat the only repeat winner. The 2020s, despite Göransson's dominance, still show high diversity through 2026 because several other composers have broken through for the first time.</p>

