  <!-- Section 4: Era Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p>Film music history naturally divides into four broad eras, each defined by the composer community, orchestral language, and types of films that dominated the award.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card era-golden">
        <h4>The Golden Age</h4>
        <div class="era-range">1934 – Early 1960s</div>
        <p>Dominated by European émigrés who brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. Max Steiner, often called the "father of film music," set the template with sweeping, emotionally explicit scores. Erich Wolfgang Korngold brought operatic grandeur; Dimitri Tiomkin infused Russian-born dramatic intensity; Miklós Rózsa lent archaeological authenticity to historical epics; Franz Waxman delivered psychological nuance. Alfred Newman, a rare American-born figure in this cohort, shaped the studio system's musical identity at 20th Century Fox.</p>
        <div class="era-stat">Key figures: Steiner (3 wins), Tiomkin (3), Rózsa (2), Waxman (2), Newman (2) &bull; 7 repeat winners across the era</div>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card era-silver">
        <h4>The Silver Age</h4>
        <div class="era-range">Early 1960s – Early 1980s</div>
        <p>The mold began to crack. Maurice Jarre's <em>Lawrence of Arabia</em> and <em>Doctor Zhivago</em> proved that leanness and thematic economy could out-epic the maximalists. Henry Mancini brought jazz to the podium with <em>Breakfast at Tiffany's</em>. John Barry's lush romanticism defined the Bond era and beyond. Nino Rota's <em>Godfather</em> melodies became cultural touchstones. By the late 1970s, Vangelis's synthesizer-dominated <em>Chariots of Fire</em> signaled that electronic textures could compete with — and beat — full orchestras.</p>
        <div class="era-stat">Key figures: Jarre (3 wins), Barry (2 wins in era), Williams (2), Rota (2) &bull; First electronic score wins in 1981</div>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card era-modern">
        <h4>The Modern Era</h4>
        <div class="era-range">Early 1980s – 2000s</div>
        <p>John Williams redefined the blockbuster score with <em>Jaws</em>, <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>E.T.</em>, and <em>Schindler's List</em>, forging the leitmotif-rich language that would dominate Hollywood for decades. Alan Menken's Disney Renaissance trilogy (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>) revived the integrated musical. Hans Zimmer's <em>The Lion King</em> brought African choral textures and electronic-hybrid orchestration into the mainstream. Howard Shore's <em>Lord of the Rings</em> scores extended the Williams-style leitmotif tradition into fantasy epic territory.</p>
        <div class="era-stat">Key figures: Williams (4 wins total), Menken (3), Barry (2 in era), Shore (2) &bull; Animation and blockbuster genres dominate</div>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card era-contemporary">
        <h4>The Contemporary Era</h4>
        <div class="era-range">2000s – 2026</div>
        <p>Globalization and genre-blurring define the current landscape. Tan Dun's <em>Crouching Tiger</em> and A.R. Rahman's <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em> brought non-Western musical idioms to the podium. Gustavo Santaolalla's guitar-driven minimalism won back-to-back for <em>Brokeback Mountain</em> and <em>Babel</em>. Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross crossed over from industrial rock. Hildur Guðnadóttir became the first woman to win Best Original Score for <em>Joker</em>. And Ludwig Göransson has amassed four wins in eight years — the fastest ascent in Oscar scoring history.</p>
        <div class="era-stat">Key figures: Göransson (4 wins), Desplat (2), Reznor/Ross (2 each), Santaolalla (2) &bull; First woman winner; electronic and global voices rise</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Shifts in Film Type</h3>
    <p>Golden Age winners were overwhelmingly dramas, historical epics, and melodramas. The Silver Age broadened to include war films, spy thrillers, and the first musical. The Modern Era saw animation and science-fiction/fantasy become dominant genres — six of the ten 1990s winners could be classified as genre films. The Contemporary Era has continued this trend while adding international cinema, superhero films (<em>Black Panther</em>), and streaming-first productions to the winning mix.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 5: Milestones -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li data-year="1935"><strong>First repeat winner:</strong> Max Steiner, who won for <em>The Informer</em> in 1935 and again for <em>Now, Voyager</em> in 1943, establishing that the Academy would reward continuity of excellence.</li>
      <li data-year="1939"><strong>Most composers on a single win:</strong> <em>Stagecoach</em> credited four composers — Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken — the most in the dataset.</li>
      <li data-year="1950–51"><strong>First back-to-back winner:</strong> Franz Waxman won consecutive awards for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em>, the first time a composer won two years in a row.</li>
      <li data-year="1961"><strong>Jazz arrives:</strong> Henry Mancini's win for <em>Breakfast at Tiffany's</em> was the first time a jazz-inflected score took the prize, opening the door for non-orchestral idioms.</li>
      <li data-year="1981"><strong>First electronic win:</strong> Vangelis's all-synthesizer score for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> broke the orchestral monopoly, proving electronic music could win the Academy's top scoring honor.</li>
      <li data-year="1989–92"><strong>The Menken trilogy:</strong> Alan Menken won three times in four years for Disney's Renaissance, the most concentrated run of wins by any composer until Göransson.</li>
      <li data-year="2005–06"><strong>Minimalism wins back-to-back:</strong> Gustavo Santaolalla's sparse, guitar-driven scores for <em>Brokeback Mountain</em> and <em>Babel</em> proved that restraint could be as powerful as orchestral maximalism.</li>
      <li data-year="2010"><strong>Rock crossover:</strong> Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross, best known for industrial rock (Nine Inch Nails), won for <em>The Social Network</em>, signaling the Academy's openness to musicians from outside film scoring tradition.</li>
      <li data-year="2019"><strong>First woman wins:</strong> Hildur Guðnadóttir won for <em>Joker</em>, becoming the first woman to receive the Best Original Score Oscar in the category's 86-year history.</li>
      <li data-year="2021"><strong>Longest gap between wins:</strong> Hans Zimmer's win for <em>Dune</em> came 27 years after his first win for <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) — the longest such interval in the dataset.</li>
      <li data-year="2023–26"><strong>Göransson's record pace:</strong> Ludwig Göransson accumulated four wins in just eight years (2018–2026), the fastest any composer has reached that mark. His three wins in the 2020s alone (2023, 2024, 2026) make him the decade's dominant figure.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

    <ol class="takeaway-list">
      <li><strong>Composer dominance is cyclical, not static.</strong> Each era has its defining figure — Steiner in the Golden Age, Williams in the Modern Era, Göransson today — but no single composer has maintained dominance across era boundaries. The baton passes roughly every 15–20 years, suggesting that the Academy's tastes evolve in generational waves.</li>
      <li><strong>The field is diversifying rapidly.</strong> From an overwhelmingly European-born, male, orchestral-composer cohort in the 1930s–1950s, the winning pool has expanded to include women, non-Western musicians, electronic artists, and rock musicians. The 2010s achieved the highest parity (10 unique winners for 10 ceremonies), and the 2020s are on a comparable track.</li>
      <li><strong>Genre boundaries have dissolved.</strong> What constitutes a "winning score" has broadened beyond recognition. The leap from Max Steiner's wall-to-wall orchestration to Santaolalla's single-guitar motifs to Reznor and Ross's ambient electronics represents not just stylistic drift but a fundamental redefinition of what film scoring can be.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeat winners are becoming rarer relative to the total pool.</strong> The repeat winner rate of 28.0% understates the difficulty of winning twice: 78.5% of all winning composers have won exactly once. In the Contemporary Era, only Göransson has achieved sustained dominance, and he benefits from the Academy's recent willingness to reward genre-spanning work (superhero films, biopics, period dramas).</li>
      <li><strong>The late-2020s will test whether Göransson's pace is sustainable or anomalous.</strong> No composer has ever won five times. If Göransson wins again before 2030, he would become the sole record holder and cement the 2020s as the most composer-concentrated decade since the 1950s. Alternatively, the Academy may pivot toward new voices — as it has at the end of every previous era — making the next several years a fascinating inflection point.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

